 \subsection{Cross-Platform Verification of the Contact-Driven Rhythm Core}\label{sec:crossengine_methods}
 
-Because the neuromechanical toolchain assembled here spans several simulation engines, a verification protocol was defined around the contact-driven rhythm core of the two-layer W2L walker architecture: four persistent-sodium half-centers (one extensor/flexor pair per leg) plus graded pattern-formation and sensory cells, driven by heel-contact mechanosensory feedback. The same core was implemented twice from a common connectivity specification --- as a NumPy reference integration through the SNS-Toolbox backend, and as native Simulink blocks from the SNS library of Section~\ref{sec:preliminary_sim_methods} --- and both were driven with an identical protocol: a \qty{12}{\second} run at a \qty{2}{\milli\second} neural step, tonic drive with a brief kickoff pulse, and alternating \qty{1}{\Hz} heel-contact trains. Agreement was scored on rhythm-generator burst period, left--right antiphase correlation, burst counts, and full-trace correlation against the NumPy reference. The archived AnimatLab reference models (the bilateral contact-driven model, the modern W2L model, and the Li walker) were additionally re-run headless from their standalone simulators with instrumented charts, so that engine-level agreement and network-level agreement could be separated. The protocol covers the rhythm core and the archived models only; full-network and whole-body behavior are reported as limitations alongside the results in Section~\ref{sec:preliminary_sim_results}.
+Because the toolchain spans several simulation engines, a verification protocol was defined around the contact-driven rhythm core of the Walker-2-Layer (W2L) bipedal architecture: four rhythm-generator half-centers, one extensor/flexor pair per leg, plus graded pattern-formation and sensory cells, driven by heel-contact mechanosensory feedback. Each half-center is built from spiking neurons whose persistent sodium current, a slowly inactivating inward current, terminates each burst, so the mutually inhibiting extensor and flexor populations of a pair alternate \citep{deng_neuromechanical_2019}. The same core was implemented twice from a common connectivity specification, once as a NumPy reference integration through the SNS-Toolbox backend and once as native Simulink blocks from the library of Section~\ref{sec:preliminary_sim_methods}, and both were driven with an identical protocol: a \qty{12}{\second} run at a \qty{2}{\milli\second} neural step, tonic drive with a brief kickoff pulse, and alternating \qty{1}{\Hz} heel-contact trains. Agreement was scored on rhythm-generator burst period, left--right antiphase correlation, burst counts, and full-trace correlation against the NumPy reference. The archived AnimatLab reference models (the bilateral contact-driven model, the modern W2L model, and the Li walker) were additionally re-run headless from their standalone simulators with instrumented charts, so that engine-level agreement and network-level agreement could be separated. The protocol covers the rhythm core and the archived models only; full-network and whole-body behavior are reported as limitations alongside the results in Section~\ref{sec:preliminary_sim_results}.
 
