 \subsection{Spiking-Neuron Mirrors of the Simulation Models}\label{sec:spiking_methods}
 
-The controllers documented above all compute with graded (non-spiking) neurons, in which the membrane potential itself is the analog activity variable that drives the plant. To test that the conclusions drawn from those models are not artifacts of graded coding, spiking-neuron mirrors of all three simulation families were built, following one hybrid doctrine: the motor-neuron layer stays non-spiking, because the musculoskeletal plant consumes a continuous activation command, while the afferent encoders, rhythm-generator half-centers, pattern-formation cells, interneurons, and commissural cells become leaky integrate-and-fire neurons operating at real membrane-potential ranges --- spikes above, analog voltages into the plant \citep{szczecinski_perspective_2023, nourse_sns_2023}. Event-based coding is expected to matter most in the afferent and rhythm layers, where mechanosensor onsets and phase transitions are discrete events, and least at the motor-neuron output, which the muscle activation dynamics low-pass filter anyway.
+The controllers documented above all compute with graded (non-spiking) neurons, in which the membrane potential itself is the analog activity variable that drives the musculoskeletal model. To test that the conclusions drawn from those models are not artifacts of graded coding, spiking-neuron mirrors of all three simulation families were built, following one design rule: the motor-neuron layer stays non-spiking, because the musculoskeletal model consumes a continuous activation command, while the afferent encoders (the neurons that convert sensor signals into synaptic input), rhythm-generator half-centers, pattern-formation cells, interneurons, and commissural (cross-side) cells become leaky integrate-and-fire neurons operating at real membrane-potential ranges, so that the network carries spikes above the motor-neuron layer and analog voltages below it \citep{szczecinski_perspective_2023, nourse_sns_2023}. Event-based coding is expected to matter most in the afferent and rhythm layers, where mechanosensor onsets and phase transitions are discrete events, and least at the motor-neuron output, which the muscle activation dynamics low-pass filter anyway.
 
-The MuJoCo--SNS-Toolbox mirror (\texttt{build\_network\_spiking.py}) reproduces the production spinal network of Section~\ref{sec:preliminary_sim_methods} population for population and connection for connection, and is selected at build time by a single environment-variable switch so that the default build path is untouched; that the non-spiking pipeline is unchanged was proven by re-running its defaults gate and baseline evaluation to bit-exact agreement. Because SNS-Toolbox 1.5.2 ships no spiking bursting-neuron class, the rhythm-generator half-centers are adapting integrate-and-fire cells: burst termination is carried by spike-frequency adaptation of the firing threshold, whose time constant reuses the analog network's persistent-sodium inactivation value so that tuned adaptation values transfer between the two builds. Spiking cells rest at \qty{-70}{\mV}, spike at \qty{-50}{\mV}, and reset to \qty{-60}{\mV}, with a tonic bias current parking their equilibrium a few millivolts below threshold --- the analog cells' near-threshold operating point. The network sub-steps at \qty{0.5}{\ms} (four sub-steps inside each \qty{2}{\ms} plant step) so that spike timing is resolved without changing the plant integration, and non-spiking readout taps are appended for the runner's stance gates and neural logging so that all existing analysis code reads analog levels unchanged.
+The MuJoCo--SNS-Toolbox mirror (\texttt{build\_network\_spiking.py}) reproduces the production spinal network of Section~\ref{sec:preliminary_sim_methods} population for population and connection for connection, and is selected at build time by a single environment-variable switch so that the default build path is untouched; that the non-spiking pipeline is unchanged was proven by re-running its defaults gate and baseline evaluation to bit-exact agreement. Because SNS-Toolbox 1.5.2 ships no spiking bursting-neuron class, the rhythm-generator half-centers are adapting integrate-and-fire cells: burst termination is carried by spike-frequency adaptation of the firing threshold, whose time constant reuses the corresponding slow-inactivation value of the non-spiking build so that tuned adaptation values transfer between the two. Spiking cells rest at \qty{-70}{\mV}, spike at \qty{-50}{\mV}, and reset to \qty{-60}{\mV}, with a tonic bias current parking their equilibrium a few millivolts below threshold, the analog cells' near-threshold operating point. The network sub-steps at \qty{0.5}{\ms} (four sub-steps inside each \qty{2}{\ms} physics step) so that spike timing is resolved without changing the physics integration, and non-spiking readout taps are appended for the runner's stance gates and neural logging so that all existing analysis code reads analog levels unchanged.
 
 The mirror's synaptic weights are set by a calibration script rather than by hand. Spikes onto the non-spiking motor neurons are calibrated by mean conductance, so that a steady presynaptic train reproduces the non-spiking steady-state motor-neuron voltage (calibration residuals at or below \qty{3e-4}{\mV}); synapses between spiking cells are calibrated by firing-rate transfer (a \qty{40}{\hertz} source drives its target at \qty{40}{\hertz}, and a calibrated inhibitory synapse silences a \qty{40}{\hertz}-driven cell); the graded-to-spiking command pathway is scaled from the measured afferent current-to-rate map. The full calibration of record, with the measured residual of every factor, is tabulated in Appendix~\ref{app:neuron_equations} (Table~\ref{tab:app_spk_cal}).
 
-In the Simulink family, three blocks were added additively to the SNS library of Section~\ref{sec:preliminary_sim_methods} (fifteen blocks at the time of writing): a \texttt{SpikingNeuron} with the same internal synaptic summation as the non-spiking block, an event-wired \texttt{SpikingSynapse} whose conductance jumps by a fixed increment per presynaptic spike and saturates at its maximum, and a \texttt{HybridSpikingSynapse} that carries the same conductance dynamics onto a non-spiking postsynaptic cell. The blocks were verified against the analytic single-spike conductance decay (maximum deviation \qty{6.2e-07}{\micro\siemens}), against a verbatim port of the SNS-Toolbox update equations on a hybrid two-neuron circuit (identical spike count and maximum spike-time deviation \qty{11.75}{\micro\second}), and against the SNS-Toolbox numpy backend itself (mean interspike-interval difference \qty{0.19}{\micro\second}). Spiking twins of the knee-reflex and beverage-holding demonstrations were then built by copy-and-patch: only the sensory interneuron layer and its synapses are replaced by spiking cells, while the motor neurons, muscles, joints, and measurements are identical to the committed demonstrations, and the twin is run at an operating point matched to the baseline gains.
+In the Simulink family, three blocks were added additively to the SNS library of Section~\ref{sec:preliminary_sim_methods} (for fifteen blocks in total): a \texttt{SpikingNeuron} with the same internal synaptic summation as the non-spiking block, an event-wired \texttt{SpikingSynapse} whose conductance jumps by a fixed increment per presynaptic spike and saturates at its maximum, and a \texttt{HybridSpikingSynapse} that carries the same conductance dynamics onto a non-spiking postsynaptic cell. The blocks were verified against the analytic single-spike conductance decay (maximum deviation \qty{6.2e-07}{\micro\siemens}), against a verbatim re-implementation of the SNS-Toolbox update equations on a hybrid two-neuron circuit (identical spike count and maximum spike-time deviation \qty{11.75}{\micro\second}), and against the SNS-Toolbox numpy backend itself (mean interspike-interval difference \qty{0.19}{\micro\second}). Spiking twins of the knee-reflex and beverage-holding demonstrations were then built by copy-and-patch: only the sensory interneuron layer and its synapses are replaced by spiking cells, while the motor neurons, muscles, joints, and measurements are identical to the committed demonstrations, and the twin is run at an operating point matched to the baseline gains.
+
+In the AnimatLab family, spiking copies of the runnable standalone exports were produced by a conversion script that rewrites copies of the model files, leaving every original file untouched: neuron thresholds are moved from the graded regime (unreachably positive) into the spiking regime, every graded chemical synapse type is re-emitted as a per-spike synapse with the same identity and amplitude (interpreted as a per-spike conductance increment with \qty{10}{\ms} decay), and the per-connection class tag that AnimatLab stores independently of the synapse type is flipped to match. A calibrated variant sets each cell class's threshold just below its measured graded peak, and an ablation that converts the thresholds but keeps the graded synapses isolates which change does what. Each copy passes a structural validator (neuron, connection, synapse-type, chart, and stimulus sets identical to its original, and every class tag consistent) and runs headless to its configured end. The 2023 spiking walker model of the same lineage, which was tuned as a rate code from birth, serves as the spiking reference baseline for this family.
 
